Rialo is addressing one of the most challenging issues facing the modern infrastructure today on how to remain secure while guaranteeing that everything remains correct while keeping everyone’s data private. With the increasing amount of sensitive information in the marketplace and the continued increase in attack vectors being developed against these very systems, the advent of blockchain technology has provided a very secure way of maintaining the integrity of data but due to the “public-by-default” nature of the technology, this cannot be utilised for all Web2 Services nor does it protect confidential information in any way. As a result, this gap is a major cause of the slow growth of Web3.

The role of Rialo is made even more critical as Jan Camenisch has joined the team. Camenisch’s research into anonymous credentials, secure multi-party computation, and privacy-preserving identity has created modern-day cryptography. Many of the current concepts behind Zero-Knowledge Systems and Decentralized Identity have their origins in Jan’s research.

Rialo’s ability to take this research and move forward with the private and verifiable computing, which is highly sought after in both the Web2 and Web3 worlds. This opens up opportunities for encrypted API Keys, Private User Data and Sensitive Business Logic to operate safely within secure environments and preserve the control and transparency of the environment. Additionally, this provides an avenue to provide true privacy on-chain and to be able to safely interface with Web2 systems.

Rialo is creating a foundation that allows developers to build robust applications that are usable in the real world without needing to compromise on either trust or privacy. The addition of Jan to the mission has created a clear path.
